{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"agent_message","text":"The retained SEC index provides filing dates and accession IDs for both reports and the 2025 proxy. It does not establish the historical availability of the mirrored annual PDF. The annual cash-flow opening balance also includes restricted cash, so using balance-sheet cash alone would produce a wrong reconciliation.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '206,270p' docs/design/chapters/13-reference-company.tex; sed -n '1916,1933p' docs/design/cases/apple/annual-2024.txt; sed -n '1732,1755p' docs/design/cases/apple/interim-2025q1.txt; sed -n '995,1090p' docs/design/cases/apple/interim-2025q1.txt\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '206,270p' docs/design/chapters/13-reference-company.tex; sed -n '1916,1933p' docs/design/cases/apple/annual-2024.txt; sed -n '1732,1755p' docs/design/cases/apple/interim-2025q1.txt; sed -n '995,1090p' docs/design/cases/apple/interim-2025q1.txt\"","aggregated_output":"\\section{Apple: source labels do not identify exposure}\n\\label{sec:apple}\n\nThe real reference package is Apple's 2024 Form 10-K and first-quarter 2025\nForm 10-Q, for fiscal dates 28 September and 28 December 2024 respectively\n\\citep{apple2024,apple2025q1}. The annual balance sheet also supplies\n30 September 2023. The annual filing is archived as the company's PDF from\nAnnualReports; the interim filing is archived from SEC EDGAR with its inline\nXBRL. Apple's Note 1 describes a 52/53-week year ending on the last Saturday\nof September. Calendar-year assumptions would therefore misstate the periods.\n\nThe package inventory preserves every annual PDF page and every interim\ntop-level section, all thirteen annual notes and all ten interim notes.\nIt records internal cross-references and externally incorporated material,\nincluding the proxy statement, as distinct obligations. Statement and debt\nfacts below are structured; policies, other notes and exhibits retain their\nsource locations and specific modelling obligations. This is complete\ninventory coverage of the acquired files, with partial semantic formalisation.\nIt does not claim to reconstruct every Apple contract or to have acquired\nevery incorporated external document.\n\nApple's annual balance sheet reports assets 364,980, liabilities 308,030 and\nequity 56,950, in millions of US dollars. Note 9 reports term-debt principal\n97,341, unamortised discount/premium and issuance costs of $-321$, and hedge\naccounting adjustments of $-358$. Thus its carrying amount is\n$97{,}341-321-358=96{,}662$, split into current 10,912 and non-current\n85,750. Fair value is separately disclosed as 88.4 billion. None of these\nthree measurements may replace another merely because a table calls it debt.\n\nNote 9 identifies the notes as fixed-rate, then states that interest-rate\nswaps effectively convert a portion to floating rates. Note 4 reports\ninterest-rate contract notional of 14,575 at the annual date. A notional is\nneither a liability carrying amount nor sufficient information for an exact\ninterest sensitivity: reset timing, remaining periods, curve conventions and\nhedge coverage still matter. The cross-reference connects the derivative to\nthe borrowing without erasing either contract.\n\nAt the interim date, total term-debt carrying amount is\n$10{,}848+83{,}956=94{,}804$. Cash is 30,299, total assets 344,085,\nliabilities 277,327 and equity 66,758. The interim debt note's rounded\n``94.8 billion'' and the statement's 94,804 million can coexist; their\nprecision differs. Treating them as conflicting exact numbers would create\nan artificial infeasibility.\n\nTo demonstrate a source-to-forecast calculation, freeze the interim balance\nsheet and apply a \\emph{marginal} financing stress for a following 13-week\nperiod: a 200-basis-point rate increase, no principal change, no offsetting\nhedge beyond the explicitly selected exposure, immediate cash settlement and\nno tax effect. An assumed effective floating exposure $E=14{,}575$ million\nproduces $0.02\\times(13/52)E=72.875$ million additional interest. This exposure\nuses the annual notional as a conditional input; it is not asserted to be the\ninterim net sensitivity. Under that condition, debit interest expense and\ncredit cash by 72.875. The resulting marginal statements have cash 30,226.125,\nassets 344,012.125, liabilities 277,327, equity 66,685.125, profit and comprehensive\nincome of $-72.875$, and operating cash flow of $-72.875$ relative to the frozen\nbaseline. The balance-sheet difference equals the equity difference exactly.\n\nIf exposure is instead only restricted to $0\\leq E\\leq14{,}575$, the\nconditional added interest lies in $[0,72.875]$ before monetary rounding. That\nrestriction is a stress assumption and is not claimed to bound every real\nApple exposure. This real case verifies evidence, context, mappings and a\ncoherent marginal propagation. Northstar supplies the complete-company\nforecast; a complete Apple forecast remains dependent on its unmodelled\noperations and undisclosed terms.\n\n\n    (1)    The valuation techniques used to measure the fair values of the Company’s Level 2 financial instruments, which generally have counterparties with high credit\n           ratings, are based on quoted market prices or model-driven valuations using significant inputs derived from or corroborated by observable market data.\n    (2)    As of September 28, 2024, cash and cash equivalents included $2.6 billion held in escrow and restricted from general use. These restricted cash and cash\n           equivalents were designated to settle the Company’s obligation related to the State Aid Decision (refer to Note 7, “Income Taxes”).\n    (3)    As of September 28, 2024 and September 30, 2023, total marketable securities included $13.2 billion and $13.8 billion, respectively, held in escrow and\n           restricted from general use. The September 28, 2024 restricted marketable securities were designated to settle the Company’s obligation related to the State Aid\n           Decision (refer to Note 7, “Income Taxes”).\n\n\nAs of September 28, 2024, 86% of the Company’s non-current marketable debt securities other than mortgage- and asset-backed securities had maturities\nbetween 1 and 5 years, 10% between 5 and 10 years, and 4% greater than 10 years. As of September 28, 2024, 14% of the Company’s non-current mortgage-\nand asset-backed securities had maturities between 1 and 5 years, 9% between 5 and 10 years, and 77% greater than 10 years.\n\nThe Company’s investments in marketable debt securities have been classified and accounted for as available-for-sale. The Company classifies marketable debt\nsecurities as either current or non-current based on each instrument’s underlying maturity.\n\nNote 6 –\nDebt\nCommercial Paper\nThe Company issues unsecured short-term promissory notes pursuant to a commercial paper program. The Company uses net proceeds from the commercial paper program for general corporate purposes, including dividends and share repurchases. As of December 28, 2024 and September 28, 2024, the Company had $\n2.0\nbillion and $\n10.0\nbillion of commercial paper outstanding, respectively.\nTerm Debt\nAs of December 28, 2024 and September 28, 2024, the Company had outstanding fixed-rate notes with varying maturities for an aggregate carrying amount of $\n94.8\nbillion\nand $\n96.7\nbillion, respectively (collectively the “Notes”). As of December 28, 2024 and September 28, 2024, the fair value of the Company’s Notes, based on Level 2 inputs, was\n$\n82.7\nbillion and $\n88.4\nbillion, respectively.\nNote 7 –\nShareholders’ Equity\nShare Repurchase Program\nDuring the three months ended December 28, 2024, the Company repurchased\nDecember 30,\n2023\nCash, cash equivalents, and restricted cash and cash equivalents, beginning balances\n$\n29,943\n$\n30,737\nOperating activities:\nNet income\n36,330\n33,916\nAdjustments to reconcile net income to cash generated by operating activities:\nDepreciation and amortization\n3,080\n2,848\nShare-based compensation expense\n3,286\n2,997\nOther\n(\n2,009\n)\n(\n989\n)\nChanges in operating assets and liabilities:\nAccounts receivable, net\n3,597\n6,555\nVendor non-trade receivables\n3,166\n4,569\nInventories\n215\n(\n137\n)\nOther current and non-current assets\n939\n(\n1,457\n)\nAccounts payable\n(\n6,671\n)\n(\n4,542\n)\nOther current and non-current liabilities\n(\n11,998\n)\n(\n3,865\n)\nCash generated by operating activities\n29,935\n39,895\nInvesting activities:\nPurchases of marketable securities\n(\n6,124\n)\n(\n9,780\n)\nProceeds from maturities of marketable securities\n15,967\n13,046\nProceeds from sales of marketable securities\n3,492\n1,337\nPayments for acquisition of property, plant and equipment\n(\n2,940\n)\n(\n2,392\n)\nOther\n(\n603\n)\n(\n284\n)\nCash generated by investing activities\n9,792\n1,927\nFinancing activities:\nPayments for taxes related to net share settlement of equity awards\n(\n2,921\n)\n(\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
